<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chełmża</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Culmsee</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chełmża, in German Culmsee, is a town in the Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>. From the 13th century it held the cathedral of the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Culm</a>, created in 1243 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>'s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>; the Gothic cathedral of the Holy Trinity survives. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> themselves resided at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobau">Löbau</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chelmza.jpg" alt="Chełmża" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kazimierz Mendlik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Che%C5%82m%C5%BCa_-_widok_rynku._-_panoramio_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
